                <pb facs="0633" n="539" sameAs="frus:frus1887/pg539"/>

                <head>No. 339.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Sir L. S. Sackville
                            West to Mr. Bayard.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1887-05-17"><hi rendition="#italic">May</hi> 17, 1887</date>.
                        (Received May 18.)</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to my note of the 25th
                    ultimo and to your reply of the 7th instant, I have the honor to inform you that
                    Her Majesty’s Government intimate that the intending emigrants are not paupers,
                    but crofters, whose passages are only partly paid from public funds, and that
                    Her Majesty’s Government would be glad to know whether this affects in any way
                    the tenor of your above-mentioned note.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">L. S. Sackville
                    West.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
